 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Sydney rents are still rising, but much more slowly than at the peak. They are not getting cheaper, though.**\n\n**Annual rent inflation in Sydney (ABS CPI, rents)**\n- **Peak:** 8.9% in the March quarter of 2024. It was 8.8% in March 2024 on the monthly series.\n- **August 2024:** 7.1%\n- **August 2025:** 3.2%, the low point\n- **August 2026 (latest):** 3.5%. That is slightly above Australia's 3.6%... or rather just below it, since the national figure is 3.6%.\n\n**Where that leaves renters**\n- By my calculation, compounding the August year-on-year figures from 2023 to 2026 gives about 14% growth over the last three years. Adding the 8.7% for the year to August 2023 gives about 24% over four years. Those increases were not reversed, and rents are still climbing.\n- Sydney rent growth stopped slowing around mid-2025. It has sat between 3.2% and 3.5% for about a year, and the latest 3.5% is a touch higher than a year earlier.\n- Rent inflation of 3.5% is below headline CPI. Sydney's headline CPI was 3.9% in August 2026 (Australia 4.0%), so rents are currently rising more slowly than prices generally. That is a change from 2023–24, when rents were outpacing inflation.\n\n**Caveats**\n- The CPI rent measure covers all tenancies, not only new leases. It lags the market, because many tenants are on existing leases and renew at higher rents over time.\n- Asking rents on new listings, vacancy rates and affordability relative to incomes would show how hard it is to find a place right now. I haven't pulled those, so I can't say how tight the market is today.\n\nIn short, the rent shock of 2023–24 has eased, but rents sit at a much higher level and are still growing at about 3.5% a year.",
